  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to forecast the probability that by each of the given deadlines, a solution to at least one Millennium Prize Problem (excluding Poincar\u00e9) will have been publicly available, with AI contributing a new mathematical argument or proof step. The deadlines are 2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, and 2050. Let me break this down.\n\nFirst, the Millennium Prize Problems that are still open as of April 29, 2025, include Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer, Hodge, Navier-Stokes, P vs NP, Riemann, and Yang-Mills. Each has its own difficulty. To solve any of them requires a complete, correct proof that meets Clay's standards. And AI must have contributed a new argument or proof step.\n\nI know that all these problems are extremely hard. For example, Riemann Hypothesis has been open for over a century, and P vs NP is fundamental to computer science. Navier-Stokes might be solved in physics contexts but the existence and smoothness is still a math problem. AI in mathematics has had some success in generating proofs or conjectures, but a full solution with AI contribution seems challenging. \n\nWith the event requiring a correct solution and a substantive human-AI collaboration (at least one identifiable new argument from AI), the probability per problem is likely low. However, over 25 years (up to 2050), the chance of progress must increase. Also, AI might enable more rapid progress in some areas. But some problems are so abstract that even with superhuman AI, solving them might take a long time.\n\nIn 2026, just a year after 2025, it's unlikely. Maybe 1% chance. By 2030, 5 years out. Maybe 5%? 2035 is 10 years. Perhaps 15-20%. 2040, 15 years: 30-35%? 2050, 25 years: possibly up to 50-60%. But I might be underestimating. However, for each deadline, the probabilities should be non-decreasing. So, 0.01 for 2026, 0.05 for 2030, 0.15 for 2035, 0.35 for 2040, and 0.6 for 2050. Wait, but even over 25 years, perhaps it's not that high. Maybe 0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.15, 0.3, 0.5? Let me check the intervals again. 2026 is just a year, too soon. By 2040 (15 years later), maybe 15% chance. 2050 (25 years) maybe 35%? Maybe cautious estimates. Let me try:\n\n2026: 0.01\n\n2030: 0.02\n\n2035: 0.05\n\n2040: 0.15\n\n2050: 0.35\n\nBut need to make sure they are non-decreasing and account for possibility that event never occurs. That would mean up to 0.35 at 2050. That seems plausible given current state of AI and math. Maybe too optimistic, but it's hard to say.\n</think>\n\n{\"2026\": 0.01, \"2030\": 0.02, \"2035\": 0.05, \"2040\": 0.15, \"2050\": 0.35}",
